What,
- [Josh] Kind.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.980
- What kind of computation is cognition?

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.780
- [Josh] Yeah, it's a
question deliberately.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.613
So, I hope we'll have the discussion.

00:01:25.613 --> 00:01:27.330
- What kind of computation is cognition?

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.040
- Thank you.
- All right.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.170
Great, thank you so much for having me,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.430
for Laurie and Brian for inviting me.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.110
It's great honor and pleasure to be here.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.230
My understanding is, this is one

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.670
of the first in-person seminars

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:40.760
that people have had here in a while.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.810
It's also the first in-person seminar

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.060
that I've given in quite a long time.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.780
So, maybe in my enthusiasm and excitement,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.570
I try to pack in too much in this talk.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.090
So, I will talk about some thoughts

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.313
on what kind of computation is cognition?

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.950
And a lot of this is really designed

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.810
to raise questions for discussion

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.470
that then I hope we'll maybe
have a few minutes for,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.143
with the larger group and also
with the class afterwards.

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:13.360
Okay, so, there's a few
questions in this talk.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.920
That is the main one.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.970
But some questions in the
background are these ones,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.860
what would constitute a meaningful
answer to this question?

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.160
And how do we know if it's right
or even on the right track?

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.370
In the spirit of this seminar,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.770
which is an interdisciplinary meeting

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.080
between cognitive scientists

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.820
and people interested metaphysics,

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.960
I wanted to take this opportunity
to raise these questions,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.000
which are essentially questions

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.560
of metaphysics and epistemology

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.330
about how do we model the mind.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.400
I think there are really
deep issues here to discuss.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.730
I'm gonna present one approach.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.470
I'm not really gonna talk
about these questions.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:45.520
I'm gonna present one approach,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.520
which I call the reverse
engineering approach.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.620
And then I'd be very
interested to talk with you

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.360
about its pros and cons, what
makes sense, what doesn't

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.030
and what are some other approaches, okay.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.350
I also think there's
intrigued in deep parallels

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.330
between the way we think
about answering this question

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.630
and the way we think about
actually modeling the mind

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.550
that reflects some of
the special relationships

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:08.360
between cognitive science
and just more generally,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.880
philosophy of science and how we think

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.340
about what makes meaningful
scientific questions and models.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.940
But that will just be in the background

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.840
and maybe we can discuss afterwards.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.690
So, what I mean by reverse engineering

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.600
is trying to characterize
how the human mind works

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.870
in the same terms that we would use

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.233
to engineer an intelligent machine, okay.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.140
But instead of doing
artificial intelligence,

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.370
we'll be doing, let's call it

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.500
natural intelligence, all right.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.340
But our goal is to build models

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.409
that look like AI
systems in a sense, okay.

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:45.480
To me, what a meaningful
answer to this question is,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.440
I would like to understand

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.780
how the mind works in those terms.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.950
And the way we know that
we're on the right track

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.120
'cause I think none of these models

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.260
that we're gonna talk about are right,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.380
but hopefully they're, in
some ways, on the right track

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.310
is, you know, the same thing
we normally do in science,

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.140
we compare our models to data.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.380
That means we try to capture qualitatively

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:04.560
how human behavior works.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.750
We want our models to behave like people.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.060
But also, quantitatively,
the models often have

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.220
a lot of moving parts,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.170
which I can only begin to
gesture at here for you.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.530
But in order to test those
in some rigorous way,

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.370
it often helps to have quantitative data.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.300
So, I want to give you a
feel for how we do that.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.490
But most distinctively I think,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.510
our goal is not just to capture

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.910
the data from laboratory experiments,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.960
which are great and the bread
and butter, the gold standard,

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.450
but they're limited in terms
of the scope of what we can do.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.340
Our goal is also, to
build models that actually

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.870
solve the problems that people do.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.500
So, in my work and in a lot
of the work I'll talk about,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.110
we do things that basically look like AI.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.880
They are AI in some form,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.260
we implement our systems in robots,

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.510
or we have them solve AI problems,

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:50.610
the same kind of problems

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.780
that humans face in the real world.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.500
And it's essential, we think,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.730
to know we're on the right track,

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.480
that the models both give a qualitative

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.420
and hopefully quantitative
account of human behavior,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:04.130
but they also solve the problem
that humans have to solve,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.750
which gives us some reason to think

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.490
that they might be describing
the actual computations

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.540
in the mind and maybe
even the brain, okay.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.050
But those are some of the hard problems.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.100
What does it really mean to describe

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.450
the actual computations
in the mind of the brain

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.250
that I think will only
have time to discuss

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.040
in the following session, all right.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.810
Now, to motivate this kind of approach,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.120
I think it's helpful to reflect

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.380
on the current state of
artificial intelligence or AI.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.820
Whether you are in cognitive
science or metaphysics

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:31.840
or any number of other fields,

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.470
including any of the
humanities, you can't ignore AI.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:38.863
It's everywhere around us for
better and worse, I think.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.990
But I think it's also important to reflect

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.393
on the state of what we mean by AI.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.570
The way I like to put it is to say,

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.560
we have all these AI
technologies increasingly useful

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.500
and perhaps, or not perhaps,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.040
actually increasingly dangerous,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.460
but increasingly powerful technologies,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.000
but we don't have any real
AI, okay, at this effectively.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.320
What I mean by that is
we have these systems

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.680
that do things we used to
think only humans could do

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.920
and now machines can
do them to some extent,

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.230
but we don't have anything like

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.370
the flexible general purpose
kind of common sense,

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.330
the general notion of intelligence

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:14.790
that each and every one of you use

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.710
to do every one of these
things for yourself

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.240
without having to be programmed

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.220
by some dedicated team of engineers

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.130
at a big tech company
or a hot startup, okay.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.000
You just do these things.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.210
And we don't have any machine

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:28.400
that can just do all
these things for itself.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.870
That was the original vision

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.676
of the founders of the field, right.

00:06:32.676 --> 00:06:35.810
What's sometimes called a
general artificial intelligence.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.433
One of the most interesting
things about cognitive science

00:06:38.433 --> 00:06:41.930
and the study of human cognition
is how might the mind work

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:42.917
that we can do all these things

00:06:42.917 --> 00:06:45.133
and so many other things for ourselves.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.070
Now, reflecting on that gap, I think,

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:53.530
helps to motivate again
what are possible ways

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.500
to answer the question,

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:56.390
of what kind of computation is cognition

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.210
and what I think are some
of the more promising ones.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.610
I wanna just dive a little bit deeper

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:05.610
into one, very useful but
very imperfect AI technology,

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.100
and that's self-driving cars
as they're sometimes called

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.470
or autonomous driving.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.720
This is an article which I believe

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.310
is the first major press
article on self-driving cars,

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:17.950
particularly on the Google effort

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.430
which is now something called Waymo

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.100
as part of the Alphabet
corporate enterprise.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.270
I'm sure most people are aware of this.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.965
There are now many, many
car companies working on,

00:07:29.965 --> 00:07:33.450
and companies of various
sorts, AI companies all sorts,

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:37.610
working on trying to build
cars that can drive themselves.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.710
And it's probably believe the closest

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:44.200
that we've come to having actual robots

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.400
with some autonomous algorithms

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.030
that don't have a human in
the loop, except when they do,

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.180
but basically don't
have a human in the loop

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.090
some of the time.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.360
And they're out there interacting

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.340
with people in real world settings

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.210
that really have major consequences,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:02.080
including life and death
consequences, okay.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.350
So, where are we in this enterprise?

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.550
Well, this article was published

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.830
in the New York Times in 2010

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.880
and just sort of announcing
these first efforts

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.743
that Google was working on.

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:18.380
It's now, well, 11 and a half years later,

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.834
by some estimates $100 billion,

00:08:20.834 --> 00:08:23.410
$100 billion have been invested

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.810
in the enterprise of
building self-driving cars,

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:28.750
and we're still not there.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.210
This is a recent article from
just a few months ago in 2021

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.750
about "The Costly Pursuit
of Self-Driving Cars

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:35.583
Going On And On And On."

00:08:35.583 --> 00:08:38.650
And oh, what sometimes called
the long tail of problems.

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.650
All the cases that you
didn't really anticipate,

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.010
that somebody didn't have data

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.180
to learn from in their
machine learning system,

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.160
that only come up as
you make some progress,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:48.993
and then you realize, nope,

00:08:48.993 --> 00:08:51.340
there's so much more and
more and more problems

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:53.883
that we just haven't
solved, now 12 years later.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.400
Waymo again, which is the
Google or Alphabet company,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.327
well by this recent
article in Businessweek,

00:08:59.327 --> 00:09:03.400
"Is 99% of the Way There, But
The Last 1% is the Hardest."

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.600
I don't think we're 99% of the way there.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.530
I think that they've been
saying that for a while

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.680
and I don't think they
would even say that.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.053
The weird echo thing for me.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:20.600
It's not just Waymo,

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:21.940
but a number of companies

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:25.367
that have been promising robotaxis.

00:09:25.367 --> 00:09:28.017
"Where are all the robotaxis
we were promised? Well."

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.050
Yeah, they're not here yet.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.570
It turns out that we need some
fundamental advances in AI.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.100
This is a actually quite a good article

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:38.330
in terms of diagnosing, I think,

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:39.440
what some of the things that are missing.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:40.700
And I'm, not gonna go into it,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.740
but it's gonna parallel
what I'm talking about here.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.067
Or another recent article
from the Wall Street Journal,

00:09:45.067 --> 00:09:47.010
"Self-Driving Cars Could
Even be Decades Away,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.460
No Matter What Elon Musk Says,"

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.740
because AI will have to get a lot smarter.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.750
So again, I don't wanna
knock this technology.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:56.110
It's an amazing achievement, okay.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.083
That works at all.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.960
And in some sense, it makes a lot of sense

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.540
that it doesn't fully work yet

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.900
and it's hard to even
know when it will work.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:04.790
What's going on?

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.890
Well, the technology,
there's lots of things

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:08.920
that go on in autonomous driving.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:09.800
But there's a basic thing

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.230
that's been driving a lot of the progress,

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.190
which also drives most
of these other progress

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.220
in AI technologies,

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:18.530
which is a certain way
to answer the question

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:21.200
of what kind of computation
cognition might be.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.157
And you could say it's basically, oh,

00:10:25.502 --> 00:10:26.780
- Just wanna turn your speaker off.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:27.613
- Oh, sorry.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.010
- Turn my speaker off.
- Yes.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:32.943
- There's no audio? Okay.

00:10:32.943 --> 00:10:34.090
There we go. Good.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:34.923
- Yeah. Okay.

00:10:34.923 --> 00:10:36.030
- Okay. Perfect.
- Thank you.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.410
- It's a certain kind of
machine learning approach,

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:41.243
which you could call,

00:10:41.243 --> 00:10:42.840
it's often called deep learning

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.210
and end to end neural networks.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:45.780
But if you don't know
what neural networks are,

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.550
I'm sure probably most of
you do know what they are.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.230
And I mean the artificial
kind of neural network

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.470
that is an AI technology.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.540
These are functional mappings.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.300
Functions that map from inputs to outputs.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.180
The inputs are usually
some kind of sense data,

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:02.000
like the sensors of the car
and the outputs are behavior.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.300
It might be conceptual behavior

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.850
like recognizing a pedestrian

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:08.460
or another car or making a decision, okay.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.780
And some complex function

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:14.490
that is implemented in the machine,

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:17.240
has many, many parameters
that can be trained.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.410
They're called neural networks

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.950
because each little bit of that function,

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:24.060
what's called a unit in a neural network,

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.960
of which there could be
millions or billions,

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.100
it's often hard to keep track

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.400
of how many there are
in some of these models,

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:34.230
is sort of an abstraction of a neuron

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:37.750
or a nerve cell in the cortex shown here

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:38.770
from the famous early studies of,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:42.030
of Hubel and Wiesel and early
mathematical abstractions.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.290
What was sometimes called the perceptron,

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:47.520
going back to the early 1960s, this work,

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:50.940
that then over a series of
decades turned into increasingly

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.460
bigger models that represented bigger,

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.780
more scalable attempts to basically build

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:58.290
these large trainable functional mappings.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:02.720
So for example, in the work
of Yann LeCun in the 1980s,

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.010
this is what was one of the early

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.290
deep convolutional neural
networks that would again,

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:10.450
take in for example, a letter or a digit

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:14.743
and classify it as
giving it a label A or 3.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:17.210
A little more than 10 years ago,

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:19.440
there was a big breakthrough
of progress where people,

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:20.520
moment of progress where people

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.990
started applying these to data sets

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.560
of natural images and
scenes in computer vision,

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:26.393
like the famous ImageNet dataset.

00:12:26.393 --> 00:12:29.620
And this is the famous
AlexNet Architecture.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:34.210
And you can see that going from
a single nerve like element

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:37.150
that takes inputs and
weights and combines them

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:40.690
with a linear sum and then
a threshold as neurons do

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:43.100
to one layer, multi-layer,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.770
many, many, many layered networks.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:46.710
At this point, these systems get deployed

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.870
in all sorts of ways,
including inside, for example,

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.410
this is inside the Tesla AI system,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.750
not the current state of the art,

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:54.860
but a couple of generations ago.

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.650
But basically, the same kind of technology

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:59.490
used to detect all these
things out there on the roads.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:02.630
This is a slide from some work
of one of my MIT colleagues,

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:07.160
Jim DiCarlo and his collaborator,
Dan Yamins and others,

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.480
where they've take these models,

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.600
which were originally abstracted from,

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.610
not just how neurons in the brain work,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:17.010
but neurons in visual cortex,

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:18.510
the part of the brain that actually

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.820
is the front end to object recognition.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:22.410
And then, they've gone back,

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.600
and many others have done this,

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.000
but their work is especially well known,

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.410
and taken the same kind of models

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.060
that have been used in computer vision

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:33.150
and shown that they can be
used to make predictions,

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.270
pretty good quantitative
about the behaviors

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.440
of neurons in responses to images.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.420
So, it's a compelling story in that

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.440
these models have been used
for practical applications.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.570
And though, you know, this is,

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:48.290
I'm not focusing here
on like human behavior,

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.820
but these models can, to some extent,

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:51.310
capture some aspects of human behavior.

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:53.770
They can also capture
the behavior of neurons.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.780
And yet, they're also
missing a huge amount, okay.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.940
And this is the gap that
I really wanna focus on

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:01.120
that motivates the work that we're doing.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:02.940
Well, some part of intelligence

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.550
is probably about training functions

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:08.970
to approximate some
classifier that can recognize

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.510
a pattern like a pedestrian in an image,

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.860
so much of intelligence goes beyond that.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:15.470
All these activities which,

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.170
why sort of lump under modeling the world.

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:20.820
So, not just finding patterns and pixels,

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:22.340
but being able to actually explain

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.080
and understand what we see

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.530
or to be able to imagine
things that we could see,

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.800
maybe things that nobody's ever seen,

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:31.530
and then think about what it would be like

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.020
if the world had those things in them.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.900
Maybe set those as goals

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.230
that we could make plans to achieve,

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:39.680
and then solve the problems
that come up along the way..

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:41.150
This is intelligence, right.

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:44.600
And then, learning is actually
building these models.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:45.860
So, that might mean adjusting

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.010
or refining, a preexisting model,

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:49.900
or constructing whole new models,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.880
maybe by combining pieces of other models,

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.550
or actually, maybe even
synthesizing a model

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.310
from scratch in some ways.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:59.570
It's all these activities that I think,

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.660
many cognitive scientists
are interested in.

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:03.980
And in our work, we're trying to capture

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:06.600
these things in computational terms.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.630
So the question for me here is,

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:11.620
what computations can or,

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.940
and in a formal way, and in a
quantitatively testable way,

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:18.660
can express these human
capacities for modeling the world

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.970
and not just finding
patterns in data, right.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.460
Now, we're far from being
able to do these things

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:27.573
at the scale that Silicon
valley is ready to invest in.

00:15:29.333 --> 00:15:30.930
Maybe they would, if they knew more

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.230
about what we were doing,

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:35.810
but it's much earlier in the,

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.970
let's call it the
scaling curve, all right.

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:39.990
What we've really been
focused on in our work

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.930
over the last few years is kind of,

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:45.900
the earliest most basic sense
of how we model the world,

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.790
which I like to call
the common sense core.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:50.850
It's heavily influenced
by a program of research

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:53.300
that sometimes referred
to as core knowledge

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.200
in human cognitive development.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.250
Liz Spelke, who is one
of the leading sort of,

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.420
developers of that whole research program,

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:04.290
probably the world's
leading expert in that,

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:05.720
she's gonna be one of your speakers,

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.020
actually the last speaker.

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:09.050
So, you'll see a lot of connection

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.330
between what I'm talking
about today and her work.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:13.373
She's a friend of and collaborator also.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.820
And I think when I talk about
the common sense core here,

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:19.830
it's also deeply connected
to the basic topics

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.310
in metaphysics that I think
the class is studying.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:24.007
It's this idea that Spelke

00:16:24.007 --> 00:16:27.010
and many others have developed
in developmental psychology,

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:29.110
as well as people studying the human brain

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.200
and some amount of comparative work

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.760
with other non-human species.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.240
That, from the very beginning,

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.900
in the earliest in youngest
infants that we can study,

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:40.810
even say three month old babies,

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:42.700
and in ways that are to some extent,

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.340
but not completely probably,

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:47.703
shared with the brains of
other non-human species,

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:51.030
we are built to understand the world,

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:54.090
not just in terms of
patterns and pixel let's say,

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:56.460
but in terms of basic kinds of concepts.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.007
So, we often talk about
an intuitive physics

00:16:59.007 --> 00:17:01.770
and an intuitive psychology or objects,

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:03.170
actual physical things in the world,

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.180
like this thing I'm picking up,

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:05.270
which even if you'd never seen

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:06.240
a cell phone or an iPhone before,

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.310
you know there's a thing
I'm picking up, right.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.540
And you know that if I
were to let go of it here,

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:11.420
that it would fall.

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:13.880
Maybe there would be a sound.
Maybe there would be so worse.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:14.713
I'm not gonna do that.

00:17:14.713 --> 00:17:18.270
But okay, that's an
example of an understanding

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.790
of physical objects and some
of the intuitive physics

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:25.020
that humans have from
even at a very early age.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:26.800
We talk about intuitive psychology,

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.260
and there we mean agents,

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:30.310
that could be humans or
could be other animals,

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:33.050
or maybe a robot someday,
or a self-driving car,

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:36.390
but an entity that has goals

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:38.570
and some model of the world itself

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:42.410
and acts in some way to achieve its goals,

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.460
given its model, okay.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.610
And the idea that we understand the world

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:48.210
in terms of physical objects

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.650
and goal driven intentional agents

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.360
and their causal interactions

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:54.000
is the heart of our common sense.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.060
And what I wanna talk about is

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:00.250
how we can try to capture
that set of concepts,

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:03.233
those cognitive systems
in computational terms.

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:07.280
I'm not gonna talk too much
about the brain side of this,

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:09.380
but there'll be maybe a
little bit depending on time,

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.550
or we can talk about this.

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:13.020
So I'm mostly gonna be focusing
on the cognitive aspect.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.290
But it's very interesting
that these systems also seem

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:17.820
to be correspond to
some of the large scale

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.563
architecture of the brain, okay.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:22.800
And happy to talk about that

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:25.310
and what the metaphysics of that

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:27.440
might be telling us as well.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.670
The work here is also, I mean,

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.920
for people who are
familiar with the different

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.520
sort of, subfields of cognitive science,

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:34.700
one of the reasons why I like it is

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:36.350
it's really at the intersection

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.500
of the different
traditional subdisciplines

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:40.810
perception, language, action planning.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.810
This is the basic stuff that
brings all those together.

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:45.520
The targets of perception,

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:48.290
the substrate of action planning

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:51.050
and also the substrate
of what we talk about,

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:52.110
at least at the very beginning,

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:53.390
when we're first learning language, right.

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:55.420
We human adults have the ability

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:57.560
to do what I'm doing right
now and what you're doing,

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:00.200
which is communicate, talk and understand

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.140
about things that are not part

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:05.300
of our basic understanding
of objects like this

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:08.590
and agents that pursue
goals in the world, okay.

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:09.990
We can talk about talking about that,

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.200
talk about modeling that as
well as quantum mechanics

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:14.310
and the origin of the universe
and the origin of life

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:16.580
and metaphysics and so many other topics.

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:19.530
But in some sense, this
is where it starts, okay.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:20.920
So to get a little bit more concrete,

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:24.860
by intuitive physics in
young children, I mean,

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.210
the kind of thing that say,
this baby is doing here,

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.560
this one and a half year old,

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.080
stacking up cups or
playing with their toys.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:34.340
Now, we have robots that can
do things like pick up objects

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:36.600
and do simple kinds of
stacking operations.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:37.820
But we don't have any robot

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:39.380
that can do what this kid is doing,

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:41.270
including the physical dexterity,

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:43.530
the ability to sort of
solve the little problems

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:45.010
that come up along the way

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:46.490
when you're trying to achieve your goal.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.280
A little bit of debugging going on here.

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.890
But even just to make the plan,

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:53.900
which you can see that
this kid is doing, right.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.490
Even if you haven't seen this video before

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.050
you can see the stack of three cups,

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:58.920
you can kind of guess
where this is gonna go

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:01.910
if I jump forward into the video, right.

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:04.610
To conceive of the goal, to make the plan.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:06.600
You know if we had robots
that could do this,

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:09.240
it would be quite amazing, all right.

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:10.410
By intuitive psychology,

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:14.630
I'll show one very famous
video from a very famous study

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.700
by Felix Warneken and Michael Tomasello.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.360
This is also with a one
and a half year old.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:20.940
But here the one and a half year old

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:22.550
is this little kid in the back

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:24.950
who's a participant in the experiment.

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:27.450
So, he is watching an adult, that's Felix,

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:30.690
do something that he's
never really seen before.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:32.810
I mean, this is a not crazy action,

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:33.950
but it's a little bit unfamiliar

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:35.260
if you haven't seen this video.

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:37.960
And now, watch what happens
when the adult stops moving,

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:39.113
the kid goes over,

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:41.983
opens the door, yeah.

00:20:45.151 --> 00:20:45.990
(audience laughing)
Yeah.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:48.510
So you guys are totally with me

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:50.660
'cause you smiled and laughed

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:53.210
and even awed all the best parts, right?

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:56.000
Somehow that kid is able to figure out

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.810
what this guy's doing for an action

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:00.060
that is not quite like
anything he's seen before

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:02.120
and then even figure out
how to help him, right.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.410
And you can see that helping goes on

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.670
when he goes over, opens the door,

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.640
and in some sense, like the best part,

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.310
both emotionally and
I think scientifically

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:12.660
is when he steps back and looks up

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:14.640
and makes eye contact, right, at him,

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.790
and then kind of looks down at his hands

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.440
because what you're seeing
when you see that right,

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.360
is at least a sign suggestive
that the kid has understood

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:25.060
what the adult is trying to do

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:27.230
and is maybe almost even signaling,

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:28.500
like, I think I've
understood what you've done.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.030
If, so I think I know
what you're gonna do,

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:33.210
which is now do something
with your hands, okay.

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:34.570
So again, we don't have robots

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:36.080
that can do anything like that.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.940
But imagine if we did,

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:39.430
they would be very
helpful around the house

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:40.680
as well as other situations.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.460
And it's not my goal to build
those robots, particularly,

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.200
although I work with colleagues who do,

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:47.720
but the goal for me is
to try to understand

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:49.730
in those kinds of engineering terms,

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:52.120
what's going on inside that kid's head.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:53.300
Now, I'm gonna show one more video,

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.380
which I know some of you have seen.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:58.310
It's one of the most famous
videos in cognitive science.

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:01.280
And I think it's not
unreasonable to call this

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.880
the most important two
minutes in cognitive science.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:06.300
Brian might have other things

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:10.323
like maybe one of his
experiments, but (laughing)

00:22:10.323 --> 00:22:14.230
this is the famous
Heider and Simmel movie.

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:16.080
I'm not even gonna show all one and a half

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:19.330
or two minutes of it, but
just a brief or excerpt.

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:21.740
But again, I'm sure many
of you have seen this,

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:25.450
but it really is like, if
you wanted one short video

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:26.880
that makes the questions

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.930
of metaphysics and
cognitive science, you know,

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:31.321
compelling as well as the
computational challenges,

00:22:31.321 --> 00:22:32.680
this would be it.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:34.890
This is from a study in the 1940s

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:38.150
in which people were just shown
this video of some shapes,

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:39.833
just moving around on a tabletop.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.430
And yet what they see is much more

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:46.363
than just simple shapes
moving two dimensions, right.

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:51.280
You see what looks like an
interaction between agents.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:53.700
One that is also sitting
on top of physics.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:56.983
One that is maybe a little
bit not super positive,

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.143
right, at this point.

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:05.350
Maybe there would be some
scary music if right now,

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:06.693
if this was a silent film.

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:10.053
Yeah. Yes.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:14.060
What's gonna happen?

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:24.553
All right.

00:23:24.553 --> 00:23:26.030
So there's a little bit more,
but we'll stop there, yeah.

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:28.170
So, it ends at least some happily

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:30.130
for several of the characters, okay.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.930
So what's going on in here, right?

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:36.930
I mean again, you can describe
this in geometric terms,

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:39.750
but what we actually see
is all these other things.

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:42.060
We see physical objects
as well as constraints

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:43.870
like what is solid, not penetrable,

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.290
what's fixed, what's movable,

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:46.760
that when the door locks

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.090
you see it sort of get suddenly attached.

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:53.090
You see events and causal
interactions like collisions.

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:54.350
One thing hits another and it moves.

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:56.070
but also it's pushing and shoving.

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:58.240
And that gives you an understanding

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:01.100
in terms of like agents might
have goals towards each other,

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:03.230
trying to hurt or help each other

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:07.170
when one's trying to
escape or trap another one.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.230
You see relationships
like you see those two

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:10.710
as being kind of on the same side,

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:12.080
they're friends or something,

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:14.150
the other guys kind of an enemy.

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:17.410
You experience, or you know,
maybe your own emotions,

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:19.640
but you experience them
experiencing emotions, right,

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.220
and probably make moral judgments as well.

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:25.300
So, you know, in a sense,
this the full challenge

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.300
of trying to understand common sense

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:30.560
in computational terms is,
could we build an algorithm

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.770
that could look at a video like this,

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:36.320
or any movie of any groups
of people interacting

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.910
and make sense of it in all these terms.

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.300
Now, again, we're far from
that, but that's the goal,

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:43.520
that's kind of what we're aiming towards.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:45.350
And so, what I'm gonna talk about today,

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:48.820
primarily, are how we can
re represent these things

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:50.160
computationally and a little bit

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:52.290
about how we study them quantitatively.

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:55.420
And then, you know, at least
gesture at these questions

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:58.290
of how they might be
instantiated in the brain,

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.420
which maybe gives a different perspective

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.150
on what it might mean for the
mind to really work this way,

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:04.730
if it's circuitry in some sense,

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:06.530
implements these computations,

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.230
and then say a little
bit, maybe about learning,

00:25:09.230 --> 00:25:11.700
both how we learn, let's
say our intuitive physics

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:14.570
and also how learning can
take us beyond these things.

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:17.400
So the key computational ideas here,

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.780
and I'm just gonna try
to be mindful of the time

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:22.560
and make sure that I get to
at least enough of the content

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.370
so we can have a reasonable discussion.

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:25.250
And there's no clocks,

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:28.160
so I'm gonna just keep
referring my watch here.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.810
But if I lose track of it, just, you know,

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:32.110
let me know it's time
to stop basically, okay.

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:36.720
So, you know, to answer the question,

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:38.840
what kind of computation is cognition?

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:40.750
It's not just neural networks

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:42.930
for pattern recognition
and function approximation.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.300
Well, here's one proposal

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.453
for some of the other things we need.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:50.550
In our work, we often use the phrase

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.920
probabilistic programs or
probabilistic programming,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:55.530
which is kind of one of these jargon terms

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:56.820
like neural networks, okay.

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:59.010
It has something to do with
probabilities and programming,

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.340
just like neural networks has
something to do with neurons.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:04.650
But what it really refers
to is a whole sort of,

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.430
you call it a computational paradigm.

00:26:06.430 --> 00:26:08.470
There's math, there's
programming languages,

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:10.340
there's there's systems and platforms

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:11.770
that I know some of you're familiar with,

00:26:11.770 --> 00:26:13.620
some of you even use in your research.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:16.996
But you can think of
it as ways of realizing

00:26:16.996 --> 00:26:20.220
in practical computational systems,

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:24.070
a synthesis of several good
ideas or several paradigms

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:25.450
or broad ways of thinking

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:27.800
about cognition and computational terms.

00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:30.600
One is the neural network
pattern recognition idea.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:34.670
Another is what is the oldest
and arguably most important

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:37.550
way to think about intelligence
in computational terms,

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:40.080
which is the idea of symbol manipulation,

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:41.500
or having symbolic languages

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:44.390
for abstract knowledge
representation and reasoning.

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:45.840
In the early days of AI,

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:47.430
as well as in cognitive science,

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:48.970
that's what everybody thought about.

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:52.070
And as these fields
have had their successes

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:54.040
and their failures and gone up and down,

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:57.400
often, the so-called
symbolic approach to AI

00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:59.410
has gotten kind of a bad rep, basically.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:01.890
People have said, "Well, early
promises of AI didn't work

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.460
because at everybody thought
we should be using symbols."

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:06.790
And then we realized we
had to use neural networks.

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:10.490
But that is let's call it fake news, okay.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:11.890
That's the nice word for it.

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:15.240
Because, you know, if you had to nominate

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.290
one of these ideas to be
the most important one,

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:18.520
that's the most important.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:20.720
If we didn't have symbolic languages,

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:24.070
we wouldn't have, you
know, natural language,

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:25.600
we wouldn't have mathematics,

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:27.380
we wouldn't have all of computing,

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:29.360
we wouldn't have pro
programming languages,

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:32.420
whether it's Lisp or C or Python

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:34.550
or modern programming
languages for deep learning,

00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:35.900
like TensorFlow or PyTorch,

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:39.410
we wouldn't have anything basically.

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:42.280
So, that's an absolutely central idea.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:45.220
And we need ways of thinking
about common sense knowledge

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:49.420
that integrates learning
and symbols, okay.

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:51.080
And then a third idea, which is the one,

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.170
I guess my work has been
most associated with

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:56.300
and is one, that when I was in grad school

00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:58.100
and in earlier times of my career,

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:01.710
was kind of inarguably the dominant idea.

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:03.670
Each of these ideas have had their moments

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:06.100
of in the sun as well as in the shade.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:07.920
But the idea of probabilistic inference

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.720
or Bayesian inference
by which we mean often,

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.340
the Bayesian part of problematic inference

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.610
is kind of inverting
conditional probabilities,

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.750
specifically in a causal
setting where we have models

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:20.660
of how some things are
caused by other things,

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:21.940
let's say effects are caused

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:24.200
by underlying latent causes in the world.

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:26.560
We observe the effects
and wanna work backwards

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:29.890
to make good guesses about the
things that cause them, okay.

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:33.240
And that idea is absolutely central,

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.370
we think, in cognitive
science for understanding

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:36.700
whether it's perception or learning,

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:39.750
or how we make sense of sparse,

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:42.300
uncertain, incomplete, otherwise,

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:44.660
and otherwise ambiguous patterns of data,

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:46.720
which whether that we're
talking about perception

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:51.020
or language or learning,
you're always in that setting.

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:52.360
And probabilistic programs

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:54.050
and the discipline of
probabilistic programming

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:55.610
and probabilistic programming languages,

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:57.930
basically let us bring
these ideas together

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:01.680
to define models of,

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:03.550
well, to define scientific models,

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.350
which are models of the
models inside your head

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:08.210
for these common sense intuitive physics

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:11.720
or intuitive psychology
using symbolic languages

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.860
that support causal models
and then probabilistic

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.720
or Bayesian inference so
that we can, in a sense,

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.160
like run these programs backwards,

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:20.660
to infer the underlying
things in the world,

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:22.830
from the effects that we observe,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:25.110
or the effects that those
things in the world,

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:27.970
cause on our senses, all right.

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:31.530
And to use neural networks or
other machine learning tools

00:29:31.530 --> 00:29:34.610
to amplify and extend what we can do

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:37.710
with probabilistic inference
over these symbolic programs.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.460
If you want to learn
more about this approach

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:40.810
beyond what I can talk about

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:43.333
in just high level terms in this talk,

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:47.140
I would encourage you to
check out any of these

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:49.010
various probabilistic
programming languages

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:52.360
or this web book, probmods.org,

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:54.350
which was written by Noah Goodman

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:55.900
and a number of other colleagues.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:57.860
Noah is a Professor at Stanford.

00:29:57.860 --> 00:29:59.090
We've worked together for a long time.

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.240
I co-wrote the first
draft of this with Noah,

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:03.270
but it's gone through many
iterations since then.

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:04.660
A lot of other people have contributed.

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:06.820
And Noah's been the one
mostly carrying it forward.

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:09.960
But it's a nice introduction
to probabilistic programming

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:13.743
and cognitive models based on
probabilistic programs, okay.

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:16.530
There's another important idea,

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:19.440
another kind of computational tool

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.940
for capturing common sense,

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.997
which is this idea that I
described with the slogan,

00:30:25.997 --> 00:30:27.700
"The game engine in your head."

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:31.100
And this is the idea that
tools from game engines,

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.540
which again, I think probably
many of you are familiar with,

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:35.790
some of you probably even programing them.

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:38.420
These are tools that were developed

00:30:38.420 --> 00:30:40.557
in the video game industry
to allow somebody or a team

00:30:40.557 --> 00:30:45.557
to create a new video
game much more easily

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:47.383
than they would otherwise,

00:30:47.383 --> 00:30:49.610
in particular, often to create games

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:51.330
that have some rich immersive experience

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.900
for a player in some, you
know, three dimensional world,

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:58.900
maybe it's outer space or
under sea or the wild west

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:03.160
or dinosaurs or something
that has never existed

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:06.210
except in the mind of the game designers,

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.710
but could exist in some possible world.

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:11.690
But to create a rich, immersive,
interactive experience

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:15.100
without having to write
everything from scratch.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:17.830
So, without having to like
write all of computer graphics,

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:20.050
but still make the world look really good,

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.950
often, nearly photorealistic

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.100
and respond to what the player does.

00:31:24.100 --> 00:31:26.210
So, as the player moves around the world

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:29.140
or the images on the screen
change in the appropriate way

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:31.300
so, you feel like you're
moving through this world

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:34.190
or as you interact with the
world, it reacts accordingly.

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:36.910
Like, so if I pick up an object
and drop it, it has to fall.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:40.250
If it's glass or something
fragile, it might break, okay.

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:44.680
So, these game engines on often
have like graphics engines

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:47.210
and physics engines, as well as

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:50.060
what's sometimes called
AI engines or game AI,

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:52.630
by which they mean tools for simulating.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:55.040
It's not really AI, but
it's tools for simulating

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.060
like the non-player
characters in the game.

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:59.960
So they react in some
vaguely intelligent way

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:03.230
rather than just a robotic
way or like for example,

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:05.840
a guard at a base might

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:07.390
instead of just like shooting randomly

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:09.560
into the air when the
player is trying to invade.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.200
They might like see you and go after you

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:13.450
and you have to like hide

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:15.820
so they don't see you for example, okay.

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:17.840
Here's an illustration
of a game physics engine.

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.090
We use these a lot in our work.

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:22.570
They, in some sense, just
implement physics, right,

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:26.530
like whether it's Newtonian me
or other kinds of, you know,

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:30.060
fluid mechanics, soft body cloth physics.

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:33.010
But they do it in a way that is designed

00:32:33.010 --> 00:32:36.750
not to actually be what a
physicist wants in a sense, maybe,

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:41.330
but to just look good, look good enough,

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:43.130
to be very general, to be able to model

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:44.330
all these different kinds of things,

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:47.040
including simple systems of
a few balls bouncing around

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:48.480
and much more complex systems

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:50.420
like hundreds or thousands of blocks,

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:52.810
like in this wrecking
ball case and other very

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.750
kind of complex non rigid
or non solid materials.

00:32:56.750 --> 00:33:00.340
And to be able to look good on,

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.300
look reasonable in what
they do going forward,

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.270
just, you know, on the scale of maybe one

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:07.003
or a couple of seconds, all right.

00:33:08.088 --> 00:33:09.690
And to be able to run in real time,

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:11.710
so it can be interactive with a player

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:13.960
or maybe even faster than real time, okay.

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:16.220
Now these tools have
increasingly been used

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.790
in many areas of AI as a training ground

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:20.360
like for an AI algorithm.

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:23.390
So, there might be like a typical thing

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:26.220
that somebody might do in a
machine learning approach to AI

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:28.932
is to have a reinforcement
learning agent that like,

00:33:28.932 --> 00:33:32.271
that is deployed into
one of these simulators

00:33:32.271 --> 00:33:34.730
and then does some things and learn

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:38.090
some input output mapping,
some policy as it's called,

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:39.930
a mapping from sense data to behavior,

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:41.960
And then maybe, you'll
deploy it in the real world.

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:43.880
And it has what's called
a Sim-to-real problem.

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:45.440
It has to figure out how to go

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:47.480
from its training ground in the simulator

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:49.410
to act okay in the real world.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.710
That's a lot of what is
going on in self-driving cars

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:53.980
is the Sim-to-real challenge.

00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:55.910
And the fact that like, people are trying

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:57.080
to create in the simulator,

00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:58.520
like every possible
thing that could happen

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:01.353
in the real world and
that not possible, okay.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:03.847
But the reason why we call this

00:34:03.847 --> 00:34:05.040
"The game engine in the head.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.270
is because the idea is
that the game engine

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:10.800
is a model of the model
inside the head, okay.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.360
Not the training grounds
for a learning algorithm,

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:15.920
but a model of the mental
models that when say,

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:17.890
a kid like this one here,

00:34:17.890 --> 00:34:21.100
sees this stack of blocks
and the toy B bird on top

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:23.760
and has the ball in his hand and imagines,

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:26.440
well, what would happen if
I roll the ball forward?

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:27.720
Oh, maybe that would happen.

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:29.070
Maybe it would knock over the blocks.

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:31.730
Maybe the bird would fall. I'm not sure.

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:33.780
But imagining what might happen,

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:36.600
then allows me to decide
if that's what I wanna do,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:37.700
or maybe it's not what I wanna do,

00:34:37.700 --> 00:34:40.350
or maybe I should do something
differently and so on, okay.

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:42.850
So, the kind of models that we build are,

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:45.810
we take these kinds of simulation programs

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:50.810
for say, physics or analogous
ones effectively for agents,

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:53.700
we wrap them inside frameworks
for probabilistic inference

00:34:53.700 --> 00:34:55.040
so that we can, for example,

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:56.660
sorry that the zoom here is cutting off

00:34:56.660 --> 00:34:57.493
the bottom of the slides,

00:34:57.493 --> 00:34:59.700
but so for example, we
can build the models,

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:01.630
like what I'm sketching here.

00:35:01.630 --> 00:35:06.320
These are sketches of the models we build,

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:07.390
not of the physical world,

00:35:07.390 --> 00:35:10.130
but of the model inside the
kid's head for a physical world

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.420
or the model inside that kid's head

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:14.690
for what somebody is doing, okay.

00:35:14.690 --> 00:35:17.600
So, models that might take an
image or a sequence of images

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:19.630
and make an inference
to what's the underlying

00:35:19.630 --> 00:35:21.315
state of the world and it's dynamics

00:35:21.315 --> 00:35:24.090
that I could then predict forward future,

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:25.380
what I might see at future times,

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:28.120
maybe conditioned on my
own physical actions.

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:29.240
Or this kind of thing here,

00:35:29.240 --> 00:35:32.610
which is not meant to be a
picture of how your brain works,

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:34.190
but how your brain thinks brains work

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:36.126
or what we sometimes call
theory of mind, right.

00:35:36.126 --> 00:35:38.060
It's a standard sort of picture

00:35:39.389 --> 00:35:42.250
of an intuitive model of agent's minds,

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:45.600
where agents have some
kind of goals or desires,

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:48.390
they have some beliefs
about the state of the world

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:49.223
and their state in it,

00:35:49.223 --> 00:35:51.310
which is a function of
their perception system,

00:35:51.310 --> 00:35:53.720
they make plans to come up with actions

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.770
that are reasonable ways, efficient ways,

00:35:56.770 --> 00:35:58.640
to achieve their desires,
given their beliefs

00:35:58.640 --> 00:36:01.290
and then they exert actions on the world,

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:02.580
which change the state of the world,

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:05.580
or change their own state
and go on and so on.

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:07.270
And so this is just a way that we are used

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:09.960
to often talking about how our minds work.

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:13.130
That is, the sort of standard
model in cognitive science

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:15.670
of how even a young child might understand

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:16.760
somebody else's actions.

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:18.650
And what might go on in a scene like that

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:22.650
is observing actions, given
also your observations

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:24.380
of the world state and the agent state

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:26.460
to try to work backwards
and fill in these things,

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:29.570
to try to infer the
underlying mental states

00:36:29.570 --> 00:36:31.160
or beliefs and desires of an agent

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.440
in order to make sense of their behavior.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:37.920
So our job here is to take
these sketches and turn them

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:42.130
into working quantitatively
testable computational models.

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:43.770
So for example, in intuitive physics,

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:44.700
this is how we've done this.

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:46.870
And this is work that in our lab,

00:36:46.870 --> 00:36:47.950
we started doing together

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:50.373
with Pete Battaglia‬ and Jess Hamrick.

00:36:51.393 --> 00:36:52.880
And they're the names on these slides.

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.620
And there's a lot of other work,

00:36:54.620 --> 00:36:57.520
especially Kevin Smith, Tomar Oman

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:00.515
did key things in this research program.

00:37:00.515 --> 00:37:02.870
But I'm just gonna tell you
about one or two studies here

00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:07.373
that go back to the earlier
work of Battaglia‬ and Hamrick.

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:12.950
I should say also, some key work was done

00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:15.420
by one of our colleagues
here, Ilker Yildirim,

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:16.620
who used to be in our lab

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:19.480
and is now an Associate
Professor in Psychology

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:20.990
and other fields here at Yale,

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:23.640
and continues to do really exciting work

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:25.450
that relates to intuitive
physics and perception.

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.130
So, if you're interested in this topic,

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:28.930
Ilker's one of the best people
in the world who does it.

00:37:28.930 --> 00:37:32.033
And so, I highly recommend you
check out some of his work.

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:37.440
But here's like one of the
very first models we built,

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:39.880
like more than 10 years ago at this point.

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:41.970
The idea is, we're giving people,

00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:42.830
sorry, I skipped over this,

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:45.800
we give people these like
scenes of these blocks,

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:48.820
they're kind of simulations
based on the game, Jenga,

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:50.150
if you're probably familiar with that,

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:51.780
but they're colored in this way.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:54.280
And these different stacks of blocks,

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:56.950
some of them will look to
you like they are stable,

00:37:56.950 --> 00:37:59.608
others will look like they
should be falling over.

00:37:59.608 --> 00:38:01.390
I, we might have to make a judgment of,

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:02.910
will it fall over or not?

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:04.700
Or you can make a graded judgment,

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:06.500
like on a scale of one to seven,

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:08.040
can do it either fast or slow.

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:09.510
It actually doesn't make
that much difference

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:10.890
in our experiments.

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:13.783
Actually, in Brian's
lab with Chaz Firestone,

00:38:15.089 --> 00:38:16.650
they've done sort of versions of this

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:19.640
where people respond extremely quickly,

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:21.540
you have to get a brief presentation.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:23.060
And you get pretty similar results

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:28.060
whether people are making very
fast perceptual judgements

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:31.060
or slower, more considered judgements.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:33.630
People are pretty good at this problem

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:36.930
of being able to judge
whether a stack of blocks

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:38.510
is gonna fall over or as I'll show you,

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:41.400
even to judge in a graded
way how unstable it is.

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:43.180
What does it mean to be pretty
good is actually something

00:38:43.180 --> 00:38:46.300
that I'm gonna try to
be more precise about.

00:38:46.300 --> 00:38:49.340
But the way we capture what's
going on here is we say,

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:51.490
you observe the image and somehow

00:38:51.490 --> 00:38:54.350
you have to work backwards
to the underlying world state

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:55.760
which is the three dimensional

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:57.180
configuration of these blocks,

00:38:57.180 --> 00:39:00.110
their geometry and whatever
is enough about the physics,

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.540
like mass, friction, the parameters,

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:04.260
the basic parameters of physics

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:06.760
that the physics engine needs
for its simulation, okay.

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:08.737
And we think of perception,

00:39:08.737 --> 00:39:12.220
basically perception is the
inverse arrow to this one.

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:14.800
So, given an image or
a sequence of images,

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:16.070
we wanna work backwards.

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:18.230
This is sometimes called inverse graphics

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:23.000
to figure out what was the
input to the graphics program.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:24.970
That is the thing that
would render the image

00:39:24.970 --> 00:39:27.780
from the underlying world state, okay.

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:29.930
And I'm not gonna focus in this talk

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:31.740
or really tell you at all, how we do that.

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:33.940
This is actually something
that appropriately

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:35.820
trained neural networks can be good

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:37.700
at learning this inverse mapping.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:39.500
You can also use other kinds of,

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:43.040
approximate Bayesian computation,
more sort of top down,

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:45.100
like, guess and check
Monte Carlo algorithms,

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:46.530
if you're familiar with that.

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:49.810
And again, Ilker actually,
has explored a number

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:52.700
of those things in his
work, very interestingly.

00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:54.520
Here, we're just gonna
assume that somehow,

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:57.650
given an image, you
get a reasonable guess,

00:39:57.650 --> 00:39:59.300
not perfect, but a reasonable guess

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:03.170
of the positions of these
three dimensional blocks

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:05.120
in the world and sizes and shapes.

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:05.953
And then that,

00:40:05.953 --> 00:40:09.320
it's also the state of
this physics simulator,

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:12.300
where now you can kind of
run your simulation forward,

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:14.750
your fast and rough
and ready approximation

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:16.343
to Newtonian mechanics,

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:20.610
run it forward a few time
steps and see what happens.

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:23.320
And if you take this rendering here,

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:25.640
which is not exactly the positions
of the blocks shown here,

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:28.140
but pretty close, and
you run that forward,

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.640
well, this is what you get
after a few time steps.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:33.580
Think of that as one what's called

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:36.310
posterior predictive sample
in this probabilistic,

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:39.030
approximate simulation based
intuitive physics model.

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.468
That's a lot of buzzwords, but hopefully

00:40:41.468 --> 00:40:44.360
you've seen enough of what
I've been talking about

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:46.070
to get some sense of what those words

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:47.970
are getting at, all right.

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:49.889
Here's another sample.

00:40:49.889 --> 00:40:52.960
I'll just flash back
and forth between them.

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:56.180
The difference is just the initial guess

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:57.270
of where the blocks are.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.090
This is a less good guess.

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:01.160
If you look at this image down
here, hopefully you'd agree,

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:02.830
this isn't like a crazy guess

00:41:02.830 --> 00:41:04.470
compared to lots of other
positions of blocks,

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:06.940
but it's a less good guess, all right?

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:08.880
And you know, we assume
that our perceptual system

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:11.540
is just giving us some approximation

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:14.263
to the true three
dimensional scene structure.

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:17.530
But whether you made
this guess or this guess,

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:19.520
when you run it forward
in your physics simulator,

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.170
basically the same thing happens.

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:24.190
At the fine scale detail,

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:25.890
very different things happen, okay.

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:28.240
So, the final configuration
of the blocks here

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:29.940
is quite different than the one here.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:31.760
But intuitive physics
doesn't care about that.

00:41:31.760 --> 00:41:33.030
What intuitive physics cares about

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:35.210
is just most of the blocks fell, okay.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:37.030
So, that's the basis of this model.

00:41:37.030 --> 00:41:38.680
You run a couple of those simulations,

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:39.770
a relatively small number,

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:42.420
like maybe five, seven, three depend,

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:44.120
I mean, we can try to
measure this quantitatively.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:44.990
I'm not gonna show you how,

00:41:44.990 --> 00:41:47.200
but like more than one, but not very many.

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:51.230
And that yields a fit to behavioral data

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:52.560
that looks like this here.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:53.840
Ignore the thing on the right for now,

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:55.450
just look at the plot on the left.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:42:00.350
So, what that scatter plot
is showing is it's plotting,

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:04.070
the vertical axis is plotting
the average human judgment

00:42:04.070 --> 00:42:05.410
on a scale of one to seven,

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:06.600
where the high end means,

00:42:06.600 --> 00:42:08.090
we people, think it's very unstable.

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:10.140
Those blocks are gonna all fall over.

00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:13.560
The low one means very
stable, not gonna move, okay.

00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:16.750
Each plus represents one stimulus

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:19.490
or one block tower scene
such as one of the,

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:20.720
these are three shown here,

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:23.550
but in this experiment,
there were 60, okay.

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:25.390
The plus are error bars,

00:42:25.390 --> 00:42:28.000
I think like 95% confidence intervals,

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:30.170
both for human judgments
and for the model.

00:42:30.170 --> 00:42:32.650
So, the model is shown
on the horizontal axis

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:35.530
and that from running a small
number of the simulations,

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:38.230
like I showed you and
just computing the average

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:40.260
or expected number of locks that fell.

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:43.110
And what you can see is it
gives a pretty good fit, okay.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:46.950
This is, when we say
people are pretty good,

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:48.640
that's what we mean here is that,

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:51.600
they're pretty well
captured by this model.

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:54.020
But there's another sense in
which they're not very good

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:55.750
and that's what's shown over here.

00:42:55.750 --> 00:42:58.170
This is in a sense,
the more correct model.

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:00.460
This is the same physics simulator,

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:02.160
but it doesn't have any uncertainty

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:04.500
as the first model does
in where the blocks are.

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:05.640
So, this is like what you would get,

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:07.050
if you could perfectly localize

00:43:07.050 --> 00:43:09.423
the position of every block, all right.

00:43:11.010 --> 00:43:14.620
That's why there's no error
bars in this side, right.

00:43:14.620 --> 00:43:17.180
The vertical numbers
and a bars are the same.

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:18.120
That's the human judgements,

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:21.170
but we've re plotted the
data with a different X axis

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:23.310
showing the actual number of blocks

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:25.110
that falls in each cases,

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:28.140
when you run that simulation
with the ground truth physics

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:30.500
and the ground truth
correct object position.

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:33.010
And it's a less good model
of people in the sense

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:34.920
that the correlation,
if you were to measure,

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:37.290
this is about what we
call a 0.9 correlation,

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:39.370
or explains about 80% of the variance.

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:41.460
The model explains that in the human data.

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:43.530
This is more of a 0.6 correlation,

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:47.110
which is about 36% of the variance, right.

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:48.823
So, it's much less good model.

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:52.470
But it also shows that in some sense,

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:53.610
people aren't that good.

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:56.270
If you judge by the actual correct answer

00:43:56.270 --> 00:43:58.260
that you would want in your physics exam,

00:43:58.260 --> 00:43:59.883
it would be this one, okay.

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:03.040
But what I would argue is that

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:04.510
actually, this is the more useful one.

00:44:04.510 --> 00:44:05.650
If you want to build a robot,

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:07.630
that's actually gonna do
this in the real world

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:09.560
where there's always
gonna be some uncertainty

00:44:09.560 --> 00:44:10.930
in its perceptual system,

00:44:10.930 --> 00:44:12.750
you want it to be robust to uncertainty.

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:15.640
And in a sense, what you're
seeing here is that this model,

00:44:15.640 --> 00:44:18.420
I would argue, is just
not robust to uncertainty.

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:22.250
In a sense you could say, people have,

00:44:22.250 --> 00:44:23.960
again, I'm sure you're probably familiar

00:44:23.960 --> 00:44:25.120
with visual illusions

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:27.083
and what they tell us about perception.

00:44:28.028 --> 00:44:31.300
This model shows that people suffer

00:44:31.300 --> 00:44:33.600
from what you could call
a stability illusion.

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:37.620
People see stimuli like this red dot,

00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:39.760
which corresponds to this red tower here,

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:41.530
which I'm sure most of
you think is unstable

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:42.750
and should be falling over.

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:45.210
But in fact, it's
actually perfectly stable.

00:44:45.210 --> 00:44:49.250
That's why the ground truth
model, it shows up as zero here.

00:44:49.250 --> 00:44:50.930
And there's many other things like this,

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:53.200
many other stimuli to
some extent like this,

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:54.790
where in the ground truth physics model,

00:44:54.790 --> 00:44:55.930
they're either perfectly stable

00:44:55.930 --> 00:44:57.620
or maybe just one block falls over,

00:44:57.620 --> 00:44:59.360
but people think they're
much more unstable

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:01.510
so the judgements are much higher.

00:45:01.510 --> 00:45:02.970
What we show in this work right,

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:04.950
is that that actually
is captured pretty well

00:45:04.950 --> 00:45:07.180
by this probabilistic physics simulation.

00:45:07.180 --> 00:45:09.880
It's just that the deterministic
one doesn't capture it.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:11.810
So, glass half full or
half empty, you can say,

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:13.630
well, this just shows the limitations

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:14.600
in our intuitive physics,

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:16.220
the ways in which we're not that accurate.

00:45:16.220 --> 00:45:17.900
Or you could say, well, this shows the way

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:19.600
that our system is designed

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:22.670
in its inherent computational
architecture to be robust,

00:45:22.670 --> 00:45:25.850
to inevitable uncertainties, okay.

00:45:25.850 --> 00:45:27.540
So, this is just sort of a little again,

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:30.600
little mini microcosm
of a much bigger pattern

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:33.300
of experimental things that
we've studied in our lab

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:35.060
and many others have
studied at this point.

00:45:35.060 --> 00:45:37.600
I'm not gonna go into much more detail.

00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:39.790
But you know, we can
ask many other questions

00:45:39.790 --> 00:45:41.730
like of the same model.

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:44.010
Which way will the blocks
fall? How far will they fall?

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:46.680
What happens if one color is
much heavier than the other?

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:49.770
Notice how these two towers
here have the same geometry,

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:51.070
but they get colored differently

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:53.170
and you make different predictions.

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:57.030
By seeing these towers
here that maybe look like

00:45:57.030 --> 00:45:58.650
they should be falling, but they aren't,

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:01.630
You can make judgements
about how heavy or light

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:03.720
some object might be.

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:06.170
And that, we can do many
other studies like this.

00:46:07.010 --> 00:46:07.843
I'll show you,

00:46:09.651 --> 00:46:11.360
since I'm just checking the time

00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:13.160
and I don't wanna run too much time.

00:46:15.033 --> 00:46:17.560
I'll show you just one other
kind of application of this,

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:19.650
which I think has interesting connections

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:22.450
to the metaphysic side of
things that I think you studied,

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:24.830
which has worked from Toby
Gerstenberg and colleagues.

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:26.890
Toby was a postdoc in our
lab for a number of years.

00:46:26.890 --> 00:46:30.400
He's now an Assistant Professor
at Stanford in Psychology.

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:33.630
And he's looked systematically at people

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:37.050
make judgements about
causal responsibility,

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:39.350
looking at various kinds of events,

00:46:39.350 --> 00:46:41.200
especially dynamic events like these.

00:46:41.200 --> 00:46:43.330
Literally looking at
how people look at this.

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:45.080
What you can see here is that blue dot,

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:47.220
you might recognize is an eye tracker.

00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:50.430
So it's a trace of where somebody looked

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:52.690
as they were watching these movies.

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:54.420
And a typical kind of Toby experiment

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:57.840
is what's illustrated here,
where people are asked

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:00.590
in some kinds of trials
to make a prediction.

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:02.700
They see that this is like
a billiard ball scene.

00:47:02.700 --> 00:47:03.810
I hope you can see.

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:06.540
So this is a dynamic
intuitive physics setting.

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.080
And you might make a prediction

00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:10.270
of what's going to happen when A hits B.

00:47:10.270 --> 00:47:11.590
Will it go in the hole or not?

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:13.820
Oh yeah. It just barely went in, okay.

00:47:13.820 --> 00:47:16.770
In other their trials like what's here,

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:19.070
the question is not to make a prediction,

00:47:19.070 --> 00:47:22.660
but to make it more of
an explanation or to say,

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:27.350
well, in this scene, did A cause
B to go in the hole, right?

00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:29.160
So, watch it and ask
yourself that question.

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:31.730
So, did A cause B to go in the hole?

00:47:31.730 --> 00:47:32.610
All right.

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:33.840
Well, what Toby found

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:35.620
and the reason why I'm
showing this here is,

00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:38.100
you can see just in how
people look at these scenes,

00:47:38.100 --> 00:47:39.810
depending on the question
that they're asking,

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:41.750
they look in a very different way.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:42.920
Look on the left here.

00:47:42.920 --> 00:47:44.883
When they're making a prediction, okay,

00:47:47.000 --> 00:47:50.130
they first look at C when
these things are gonna collide

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:51.730
and then they just extrapolate forward

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:53.410
where B is going, okay.

00:47:53.410 --> 00:47:55.870
But when they're doing a causal judgment,

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:58.340
so they're basically trying
to judge in a graded way,

00:47:58.340 --> 00:48:00.510
how responsible was A for making B going,

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:01.900
oh, look at where they're looking.

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:05.280
They're not just looking
at where B is going to go,

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:08.540
but where B would have gone
if A hadn't been there.

00:48:08.540 --> 00:48:09.373
Do you see, how they are sort of,

00:48:09.373 --> 00:48:11.440
trying to extrapolate B's motion?

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:14.120
And that's because there's
a long tradition of work.

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.300
Some of which is actually the core work

00:48:16.300 --> 00:48:20.180
that Laurie first became
known for in metaphysics,

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:25.010
which is looking at how the
role of counterfactual analysis

00:48:25.010 --> 00:48:27.850
in causal relations basically.

00:48:27.850 --> 00:48:29.460
And I know this is something I think,

00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:30.710
you guys have talked about in the class,

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:34.270
so I'm not gonna try to
retell in a limited way.

00:48:34.270 --> 00:48:36.500
But it's quite striking that you can see

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:38.260
in how people look at a scene

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:40.870
that they're doing this kind
of counterfactual analysis,

00:48:40.870 --> 00:48:43.090
if and only if, in very, very

00:48:43.090 --> 00:48:46.170
dramatic different proportions,

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:47.923
they're making a causal judgment.

00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:50.610
Toby can also model this

00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:54.670
with his probabilistic physics simulation.

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:56.640
And this is just a
sketch of how this works.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:48:59.010
It's very much like the block tower model

00:48:59.010 --> 00:49:00.640
that I showed. okay.

00:49:00.640 --> 00:49:02.390
Except that what the
system is trying to do is,

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:03.440
it's trying to make,

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:06.030
it has to make guesses
of the counterfactual.

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:08.920
What would've happened if
A hadn't been in the scene?

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:11.160
You don't actually know, right.

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:13.000
You can see those eye movements.

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:14.800
In a sense, those are the guesses realized

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:16.320
right there on the screen in front of you.

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:17.440
They're not perfect.

00:49:17.440 --> 00:49:19.390
They're sort of noisy extrapolations.

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:21.140
And the model, though you
can't read it down here,

00:49:21.140 --> 00:49:23.260
is basically doing similar simulations

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:24.800
with kind of noisy estimates

00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:27.740
of the ball's velocities
and positions, okay.

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:29.400
So, the same idea of a noisy

00:49:29.400 --> 00:49:32.550
or probabilistic approximate
physics simulation

00:49:32.550 --> 00:49:33.690
can be used to capture

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:35.120
how people predict what's gonna happen.

00:49:35.120 --> 00:49:37.420
And the counterfactual probabilities,

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:39.030
they have to compute in order to make

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:40.910
a causal responsibility judgment.

00:49:40.910 --> 00:49:43.210
And I won't go into the details

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:44.690
of how you study this experimentally.

00:49:44.690 --> 00:49:48.300
But Toby has done really
wonderful experiments

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:50.320
where he manipulates the degree of,

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:52.860
how close or far the counterfactual is

00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:54.500
and how that separates from the actual one

00:49:54.500 --> 00:49:56.231
and gets very beautiful fits to data.

00:49:56.231 --> 00:49:58.610
It's an advertisement for basically,

00:49:58.610 --> 00:50:01.440
really elegant work that
shows the quantitative power

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:04.520
of these models for capturing
a sense of causality

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:06.120
and causal responsibility

00:50:06.120 --> 00:50:08.973
and not just predicting what's
gonna happen next, okay.

00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:11.380
One more advertisement.

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:13.120
And I'll try to keep this even shorter,

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:14.130
but this is recent work

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:16.720
from Kelsey Allen and Kevin
Smith who I mentioned,

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:18.700
where they're adding in the next step,

00:50:18.700 --> 00:50:21.300
which is, how do you use
probabilistic intuitive physics

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:22.840
to solve problems, okay?

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.580
In this case, thinking
about the human ability

00:50:25.580 --> 00:50:29.510
to make and use tools in
novel creative ways, right?

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:30.910
Again, when we think about cognition,

00:50:30.910 --> 00:50:32.870
we think about creative problem solving

00:50:32.870 --> 00:50:34.690
as a as a core aspect

00:50:34.690 --> 00:50:37.283
of where our general purpose
intelligence comes in.

00:50:38.200 --> 00:50:39.190
Many people who studied

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:40.730
the evolution of human intelligence,

00:50:40.730 --> 00:50:43.920
focus on the human ability
to not just make tools,

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:45.860
but to just sort of find
and repurpose things

00:50:45.860 --> 00:50:48.330
like this or this rock,
or think of all the things

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:49.350
I could use this phone for

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:51.160
that aren't just making a call, right?

00:50:51.160 --> 00:50:53.060
Your phone's battery
is dead. Is it useless?

00:50:53.060 --> 00:50:54.790
Well, it depends, right.

00:50:54.790 --> 00:50:59.090
Maybe unstick this or,
you know, you have to,

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:00.350
well, I won't go into it, okay.

00:51:00.350 --> 00:51:05.280
But in this paper, which
was published back in 2020,

00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:08.080
feels like just yesterday,
like a lot of things in 2020,

00:51:09.760 --> 00:51:13.870
in PNAS, they had people playing

00:51:13.870 --> 00:51:16.080
this really cool virtual tools game,

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:17.900
where they basically have
to solve these problems.

00:51:17.900 --> 00:51:21.129
The problem is always get the
red ball into the green bowl.

00:51:21.129 --> 00:51:23.465
And there's many different
things that happen

00:51:23.465 --> 00:51:26.310
in these different levels of this game.

00:51:26.310 --> 00:51:28.280
It's kind of inspired
speaking of phones by these,

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:32.440
like touch physics phone games,
which are popular pastime.

00:51:32.440 --> 00:51:34.680
And what they show is that basically,

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:37.400
a probabilistic simulation based model

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:40.790
provides a good account
of the internal process,

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:43.640
we pose it, of people
sort of trying out ways

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:45.850
they could solve the
problem by picking in a tool

00:51:45.850 --> 00:51:48.700
and thinking about where they
might place it into the scene.

00:51:48.700 --> 00:51:53.700
It can capture both where
people choose to drop objects

00:51:53.860 --> 00:51:56.260
as well as sort of the learning dynamics.

00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:59.313
And what this graph is just showing is,

00:51:59.313 --> 00:52:01.460
there's sort of this rapid trial and error

00:52:01.460 --> 00:52:02.633
kind of learning here.

00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:05.640
Sometimes you might see people talk about

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:08.750
reinforcement learning
algorithms in AI and say,

00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:11.980
well, they learn like people
or animals by trial and error,

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:13.420
but unlike what's called

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:15.820
reinforcement learning typically in AI,

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:18.160
people don't learn from, you know,

00:52:18.160 --> 00:52:20.540
thousands or millions
of training examples.

00:52:20.540 --> 00:52:23.370
They do do trial and error
learning as we all often do

00:52:23.370 --> 00:52:24.760
when we're trying to solve problems.

00:52:24.760 --> 00:52:26.130
But the real trial and error learning

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:28.700
is something that unfolds
over like five or 10 trials.

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:31.220
And that's what we see in
this virtual tools game

00:52:31.220 --> 00:52:33.053
as well as in the model, okay.

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:35.530
So, I guess I don't have time

00:52:35.530 --> 00:52:37.280
to talk about intuitive psychology.

00:52:46.010 --> 00:52:47.900
But I do wanna advertise two things.

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:51.530
One is, if you're interested
in how these kinds of ideas

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:54.750
play out in understanding
intuitive psychology,

00:52:54.750 --> 00:52:56.280
you're again, very lucky here at Yale

00:52:56.280 --> 00:52:57.780
to have one of the world's experts

00:52:57.780 --> 00:53:00.660
in Julian Jara-Ettinger
and his research group.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:02.930
They do many things,
but among other things,

00:53:02.930 --> 00:53:05.320
they have developed models for,

00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:06.643
and sort of taken the kind of models

00:53:06.643 --> 00:53:08.200
that I've described here

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:11.310
and shown all sorts of really
cool things to do with them.

00:53:11.310 --> 00:53:14.296
And I'm not gonna go into
the details of those.

00:53:14.296 --> 00:53:18.800
But I do want to show
you the following work.

00:53:18.800 --> 00:53:20.870
And again, I guess we could just call it

00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:21.790
mostly an advertisement.

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:24.380
But since I bothered to
show you Heider and Simmel

00:53:24.380 --> 00:53:26.120
I wanna emphasize the work

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:27.770
that is completely hidden on the bottom

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:29.320
because of the zoom thing.

00:53:29.320 --> 00:53:31.670
I'm gonna just move that
for this purpose here.

00:53:33.320 --> 00:53:36.500
It's been a dream of many
cognitive scientists,

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:41.500
and certainly of mine, to ha
to be able to really study,

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:43.730
take that Heider and Simmel video

00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:46.530
and basically the build
models that can really see

00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:47.590
everything that people can do there.

00:53:47.590 --> 00:53:49.280
Now, we aren't there yet, okay.

00:53:49.280 --> 00:53:51.750
But maybe, we're like 99% of
the way. No, not even close.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:54.223
But maybe we're like 40% of the way.

00:53:55.330 --> 00:53:56.880
So, this is really exciting work

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:59.160
by Aviv Netanyahu and Tianmin Shu,

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:00.390
they're the co-first authors,

00:54:00.390 --> 00:54:02.200
which they published a version of it

00:54:02.200 --> 00:54:03.570
at a recent AI conference.

00:54:03.570 --> 00:54:05.820
But it's honestly, it's even more exciting

00:54:05.820 --> 00:54:07.820
as cognitive science than AI?

00:54:07.820 --> 00:54:09.463
It's this phase paper.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:12.070
And what they've done is they basically

00:54:12.070 --> 00:54:15.900
built this little domain
that they call flatland,

00:54:15.900 --> 00:54:17.570
where you can see these agents here

00:54:17.570 --> 00:54:20.130
that are basically interacting
with physical objects.

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:23.310
They can like pick up things,
exert forces, throw them.

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:25.390
And it allows us to capture

00:54:25.390 --> 00:54:28.670
in a controlled
quantitatively studiable way,

00:54:28.670 --> 00:54:30.420
many of the things that are going on.

00:54:30.420 --> 00:54:32.400
Not just when you see a single agent,

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:33.930
like try to pursue a goal,

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:35.660
but when you have multiple
agents interacting,

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:37.460
like in the Heider and Simmel video.

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:43.410
So in this setup, some agents
are strong some are weaker.

00:54:43.410 --> 00:54:45.380
Agents can have different goals.

00:54:45.380 --> 00:54:47.010
Like an agent could say,

00:54:47.010 --> 00:54:50.430
the red thing here could be
to get to the gold thing,

00:54:50.430 --> 00:54:52.290
or the agent could just have a goal

00:54:52.290 --> 00:54:54.150
to get to another entity.

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:56.020
The agent here could have a goal

00:54:56.020 --> 00:54:57.796
to get the blue ball to the red space.

00:54:57.796 --> 00:55:01.160
These are possible goals
that the agents could have.

00:55:01.160 --> 00:55:03.890
They also have relationships
and social goals.

00:55:03.890 --> 00:55:05.800
So, agents could be helpful to each other

00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:07.790
as you can see here, where
it looks like the red agent

00:55:07.790 --> 00:55:08.990
is trying to help the green agent

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:10.700
get the blue balls to somewhere.

00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:12.650
They could be adversarial like the way

00:55:12.650 --> 00:55:15.350
they seem to be fighting over
where this ball is to go.

00:55:15.350 --> 00:55:16.520
Or they could just be independent

00:55:16.520 --> 00:55:18.170
like the green one wants to get that ball

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:20.120
up to the green space and the red one

00:55:20.120 --> 00:55:21.590
just wants to go down there.

00:55:21.590 --> 00:55:23.140
So, these are all possibilities.

00:55:23.140 --> 00:55:25.650
The cool thing is, these
weren't made by humans.

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:27.010
These were made by our models.

00:55:27.010 --> 00:55:28.280
So, we have a model which is

00:55:28.280 --> 00:55:31.080
a probabilistic generative model

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:32.710
of these multi-agent interactions.

00:55:32.710 --> 00:55:34.720
It sits on top of a physics engine,

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:35.930
'cause you can see that these agents

00:55:35.930 --> 00:55:37.530
are interacting with physics.

00:55:37.530 --> 00:55:40.310
And each agent has its own sort of goal

00:55:40.310 --> 00:55:42.270
and representation of
its social relationship

00:55:42.270 --> 00:55:43.430
and the other agent's goal.

00:55:43.430 --> 00:55:46.690
And then they do a fairly
complex planning process

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:49.100
to generate the sequence of behavior.

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:51.560
So, the probabilistic program

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:54.180
goes from these underlying physics

00:55:54.180 --> 00:55:56.780
and social variables
and produces the movies.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:58.620
But then you can run it in reverse.

00:55:58.620 --> 00:55:59.453
It's not easy,

00:55:59.453 --> 00:56:01.400
but with the right kinds
of inference algorithms,

00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:03.790
you can then also see the
movie and work backwards

00:56:03.790 --> 00:56:06.590
to infer what those goals are,

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:08.530
both the individual ones, the social ones.

00:56:08.530 --> 00:56:10.070
And then also run it again forward

00:56:10.070 --> 00:56:12.050
and predict what's gonna happen next.

00:56:12.050 --> 00:56:14.540
And so, you can see in this world

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:15.930
with this generative model,

00:56:15.930 --> 00:56:17.610
it can produce quite interesting behavior.

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:22.490
So for example, here,
you have an agent that,

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:24.010
well, notice what happens here.

00:56:24.010 --> 00:56:25.060
You can just see what happens.

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.610
The green one goes and steals the blue one

00:56:27.610 --> 00:56:30.310
and then the red one goes
and fights him for it, okay,

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:34.180
and successfully gets it back, okay.

00:56:34.180 --> 00:56:36.320
So there, it's an adversarial interaction,

00:56:36.320 --> 00:56:41.320
but because the red one didn't
see the green one initially,

00:56:41.400 --> 00:56:44.420
he kind of left blue
one unprotected, okay.

