01:08:10.280 --> 01:08:13.600
empirical expectations over
stochastic simulations.

01:08:13.600 --> 01:08:16.340
That's what's behind a
lot of what I showed you,

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:18.922
and that can be implemented
directly in networks

01:08:18.922 --> 01:08:21.110
of stochastic spiking neurons.

01:08:21.110 --> 01:08:22.970
So, that's another route to
try to make this mapping.

01:08:22.970 --> 01:08:25.510
So, they're both like much to be done

01:08:25.510 --> 01:08:26.670
in the future to really see

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.300
if any of those are going to work.

01:08:28.300 --> 01:08:30.200
But that's, I think, especially exciting

01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:32.462
to try to relate these models of the mind

01:08:32.462 --> 01:08:34.300
to models of the brain.

01:08:34.300 --> 01:08:35.930
And I think, that's also gonna be central

01:08:35.930 --> 01:08:37.780
to really having a fully satisfying answer

01:08:37.780 --> 01:08:40.213
to what kind of computation is cognition.

01:08:41.640 --> 01:08:42.950
- [Brian] That was a
very satisfying answer.

01:08:42.950 --> 01:08:45.248
Not all of the answers can be that long.

01:08:45.248 --> 01:08:46.081
- Okay.

01:08:46.081 --> 01:08:46.914
- [Brian] Let's take some more questions.

01:08:46.914 --> 01:08:49.260
And since that was a computational

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:51.670
sort of oriented question,

01:08:51.670 --> 01:08:52.830
I believe one of the people

01:08:52.830 --> 01:08:55.520
who had their hands up still in the back,

01:08:55.520 --> 01:08:59.903
let's move to a philosophy
oriented question with a,

01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:03.650
we're not supposed to say our
names because of releases,

01:09:03.650 --> 01:09:06.100
but this person's name
may or may not be Michael.

01:09:08.030 --> 01:09:10.903
- Fortunately, we can do
probabilistic inference.

01:09:12.400 --> 01:09:14.770
- Thank you, Brian and thank you, Josh.

01:09:14.770 --> 01:09:17.330
A couple times in the talk, you mentioned

01:09:17.330 --> 01:09:20.520
you poo-poo the idea that
we're 99% of the way there,

01:09:20.520 --> 01:09:22.470
we're more like 40% of the way there.

01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:25.063
What would 100% of the way there be like?

01:09:25.063 --> 01:09:29.523
And would being 100% get
us success of any kind?

01:09:31.240 --> 01:09:33.190
So, I'm trying to envisage
what would success look like?

01:09:33.190 --> 01:09:35.740
- Right. So that, I did encourage
you to ask that question.

01:09:35.740 --> 01:09:40.160
So, thank you for asking
that difficult question.

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:41.640
I think it depends.

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:43.830
Well, wait, can you let him
keep the mic for one second?

01:09:43.830 --> 01:09:45.860
Do you mean success towards,

01:09:45.860 --> 01:09:47.593
so I used those numbers like 99%

01:09:47.593 --> 01:09:51.140
in both somebody's estimate
of how close are we

01:09:51.140 --> 01:09:53.750
to an AI goal, like self-driving cars,

01:09:53.750 --> 01:09:55.720
or also, how close are we

01:09:55.720 --> 01:09:57.600
towards some kind cognitive modeling goal?

01:09:57.600 --> 01:09:58.540
Which one were you asking?

01:09:58.540 --> 01:10:00.130
- [Student] Well, I was
thinking like the success,

01:10:00.130 --> 01:10:03.580
like you have something that
can behave just like humans do.

01:10:03.580 --> 01:10:06.090
And could they pass a
training test for example?

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:09.250
And why would that be success

01:10:09.250 --> 01:10:11.670
if this training test can be passed?

01:10:11.670 --> 01:10:12.503
That's interesting,

01:10:12.503 --> 01:10:14.890
but what does it tell
us about the human mind?

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:16.540
- Yeah.

01:10:16.540 --> 01:10:21.490
So, I don't know if I can
answer that in a short answer.

01:10:21.490 --> 01:10:22.323
And Brian has,

01:10:24.210 --> 01:10:28.100
- A haiku.
(audience laughing)

01:10:28.100 --> 01:10:31.153
Okay. This may be more
like 22 syllables than 12.

01:10:32.670 --> 01:10:37.670
But this is about scientific
model a building, right.

01:10:39.420 --> 01:10:42.120
And I don't think I'm gonna
be able to give you a fully,

01:10:42.120 --> 01:10:43.960
I don't know that I can give you

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:46.320
a fully satisfying answer
to that question at all,

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:49.210
let alone the one you want me
to answer right here, okay.

01:10:49.210 --> 01:10:53.070
But I think as in most other
areas of science, you know,

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:55.410
how do we know when our
models are on the right track?

01:10:55.410 --> 01:10:57.990
Well, you could call it
some notion of coherence.

01:10:57.990 --> 01:11:00.390
And I think this is like basic
metaphysics and epistemology,

01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:02.960
which my philosophy colleague
can tell me more about.

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:06.970
But when you have a model that
captures many different ways

01:11:06.970 --> 01:11:09.170
of getting at the same
phenomena, all right,

01:11:09.170 --> 01:11:11.770
that can include different
kinds of behavioral judgements

01:11:11.770 --> 01:11:13.320
that we can study in the lab,

01:11:13.320 --> 01:11:15.470
that can include acting like a person

01:11:15.470 --> 01:11:17.570
in the corresponding real world scenarios.

01:11:17.570 --> 01:11:19.640
That's the sort of AI tests of the model.

01:11:19.640 --> 01:11:22.160
And as we're starting to be
able to do, that can also,

01:11:22.160 --> 01:11:24.820
maybe even predict some
neural responses, okay.

01:11:24.820 --> 01:11:26.790
Which I didn't show you, that
these models are able to do,

01:11:26.790 --> 01:11:28.810
but they're starting to go
in that direction, okay.

01:11:28.810 --> 01:11:31.570
If this, if the same class
of models can explain

01:11:31.570 --> 01:11:35.790
or let's say can just fit all
those different sorts of data,

01:11:35.790 --> 01:11:38.180
and I would say, even
explain them in the sense

01:11:38.180 --> 01:11:40.510
that gives a functional explanation

01:11:40.510 --> 01:11:42.530
of why the behavior is the way it is,

01:11:42.530 --> 01:11:44.400
why the neurons are the way they are,

01:11:44.400 --> 01:11:48.310
because we would argue
this computational approach

01:11:48.310 --> 01:11:50.210
is actually solving
the real world problems

01:11:50.210 --> 01:11:52.190
in some efficient, effective way.

01:11:52.190 --> 01:11:57.190
I think that's a coherent kind of,

01:11:57.340 --> 01:12:01.030
at least epistemology here
and maybe even metaphysics,

01:12:01.030 --> 01:12:02.380
I think fundamentally, right,

01:12:02.380 --> 01:12:04.830
what is lurking behind this approach,

01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:06.680
and I can try to unpack this in less haiku

01:12:06.680 --> 01:12:11.250
like fashion later on is
that, I think at least,

01:12:11.250 --> 01:12:13.550
and I think most of us
as scientists think,

01:12:13.550 --> 01:12:16.000
the world is real, it
is really real thing.

01:12:16.000 --> 01:12:17.480
But the best we can do in our science

01:12:17.480 --> 01:12:18.690
is to build models of it.

01:12:18.690 --> 01:12:20.520
And that's true when we're doing science

01:12:20.520 --> 01:12:22.780
and it's true when we're
doing cognition also, right.

01:12:22.780 --> 01:12:24.730
This view of the mind that
I'm talking about here,

01:12:24.730 --> 01:12:26.760
and that is, I think, a canonical
one in cognitive science

01:12:26.760 --> 01:12:29.780
is this idea of the mind
as modeler a model builder.

01:12:29.780 --> 01:12:31.980
And I think the same kinds of tools

01:12:31.980 --> 01:12:34.630
of probabilistic modeling, prediction,

01:12:34.630 --> 01:12:37.030
including counterfactual
or hypothetical data,

01:12:37.030 --> 01:12:39.100
like the same things that
we think the mind does,

01:12:39.100 --> 01:12:41.630
we as scientists do, it's just the data,

01:12:41.630 --> 01:12:43.360
isn't the data coming
in through our retinas.

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:45.320
It's the data that we get when people

01:12:45.320 --> 01:12:46.500
press buttons and say things,

01:12:46.500 --> 01:12:48.260
when we put things through
their retina, right?

01:12:48.260 --> 01:12:52.180
And I think that basic notion
of coherence and modeling

01:12:52.180 --> 01:12:54.250
is at the heart of why, you know,

01:12:54.250 --> 01:12:56.360
it's heart of how we, as humans

01:12:56.360 --> 01:12:57.870
intuitively understand the world,

01:12:57.870 --> 01:13:00.310
it's what makes satisfying understanding

01:13:00.310 --> 01:13:03.780
in pretty much every area
of science that I know.

01:13:03.780 --> 01:13:07.310
And I would love to explore more,

01:13:07.310 --> 01:13:09.505
trying to make that more
rigorous argument, I think.

01:13:09.505 --> 01:13:10.830
Let's just say, okay.

01:13:10.830 --> 01:13:12.610
- [Brian] Let's take one more question

01:13:12.610 --> 01:13:15.970
before Josh has shuttled up to the seminar

01:13:15.970 --> 01:13:18.426
that he'll talk to and someone who's name

01:13:18.426 --> 01:13:20.090
may or may not be Jack.

01:13:23.790 --> 01:13:24.970
- [Student] Hi, I have a question

01:13:24.970 --> 01:13:28.000
about sort of the architecture here,

01:13:28.000 --> 01:13:30.970
when you introduced the game engine

01:13:32.110 --> 01:13:34.193
part of cognition, part of learning,

01:13:35.750 --> 01:13:37.190
is the claim there that that's sort of,

01:13:37.190 --> 01:13:39.460
a separate self-sufficient module?

01:13:39.460 --> 01:13:42.440
And if so, how does it interface

01:13:42.440 --> 01:13:45.293
with symbolic or semantic representations?

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:47.443
- Right. Okay.

01:13:49.790 --> 01:13:51.720
Because time is short,

01:13:51.720 --> 01:13:53.180
there's a lot to unpack there for people

01:13:53.180 --> 01:13:55.370
who maybe don't know everything
you were talking about.

01:13:55.370 --> 01:14:00.370
But I will just say, so these models,

01:14:02.110 --> 01:14:02.943
so, okay.

01:14:02.943 --> 01:14:06.930
So, there's I guess a standard
cognitive science view

01:14:06.930 --> 01:14:08.130
that I think maybe you're referring to,

01:14:08.130 --> 01:14:10.060
like a sort of photo modularity of mind,

01:14:10.060 --> 01:14:12.660
language of thought kind
of set of questions, right?

01:14:13.510 --> 01:14:17.030
So to translate this
picture into those terms,

01:14:17.030 --> 01:14:20.160
I would say there's not, the single module

01:14:20.160 --> 01:14:21.833
in the mind or brain
that's the game engine.

01:14:21.833 --> 01:14:24.260
. There's actually a set of brain systems

01:14:24.260 --> 01:14:25.470
that I can even tell you where they are

01:14:25.470 --> 01:14:27.710
based on some of my colleagues FMRI work,

01:14:27.710 --> 01:14:29.040
that are both, you know,

01:14:29.040 --> 01:14:31.740
somewhat functionally distinct subsystems.

01:14:31.740 --> 01:14:33.070
They also interact in ways.

01:14:33.070 --> 01:14:35.380
And like actually, a
good chunk of the brain

01:14:35.380 --> 01:14:39.760
can be mapped onto these different parts

01:14:39.760 --> 01:14:41.760
of the game engine in this model.

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:43.180
Ilker wrote a nice review of this

01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:45.550
in "Current Opinion in
Neurobiology" a couple of years ago

01:14:45.550 --> 01:14:48.370
on at least the sort of
object physics part of that.

01:14:48.370 --> 01:14:51.380
So, I'll refer you to that
paper for one part of it.

01:14:51.380 --> 01:14:53.640
But I think with some other colleagues

01:14:53.640 --> 01:14:54.660
who've been studying like basically,

01:14:54.660 --> 01:14:56.030
social cognition networks,

01:14:56.030 --> 01:14:58.200
whether it's theory of mind or other ones.

01:14:58.200 --> 01:15:00.410
So, you can actually look
at the brain and see some,

01:15:00.410 --> 01:15:04.053
both modular structure, but
also interacting models.

01:15:05.850 --> 01:15:06.750
I would say they have

01:15:06.750 --> 01:15:09.230
some interesting informational interfaces.

01:15:09.230 --> 01:15:12.750
They're not as encapsulated
as Photer would've said,

01:15:12.750 --> 01:15:16.010
but there is some functionally
distinct sub-structure, okay.

01:15:16.010 --> 01:15:18.300
But you also asked about

01:15:18.300 --> 01:15:20.880
like traditional symbolic
and semantic knowledge.

01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:24.550
So, I think a really important
part of this research program

01:15:24.550 --> 01:15:26.250
is that these models are,

01:15:26.250 --> 01:15:27.790
what I think many cognitive scientists

01:15:27.790 --> 01:15:30.440
might call like pre-semantic.

01:15:30.440 --> 01:15:31.950
They're often more like perception.

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:32.900
I mean Brian and I will argue

01:15:32.900 --> 01:15:35.340
about this probably in a few minutes.

01:15:35.340 --> 01:15:36.173
And I think,

01:15:36.173 --> 01:15:37.150
no, it's really interesting,

01:15:37.150 --> 01:15:39.690
and that in many ways,

01:15:39.690 --> 01:15:43.210
these models are more like
perception than like cognition.

01:15:43.210 --> 01:15:44.448
Even if Brian might say
sometimes, he might,

01:15:44.448 --> 01:15:46.070
I mean, I'll let you say.

01:15:46.070 --> 01:15:48.340
But like, they have some
components of the way Brian,

01:15:48.340 --> 01:15:50.850
who draws a strong line between
perception and cognition

01:15:50.850 --> 01:15:52.400
would call perception and others

01:15:52.400 --> 01:15:53.420
that are more like cognition.

01:15:53.420 --> 01:15:56.160
But they are certainly not like, you know,

01:15:56.160 --> 01:15:59.900
sort of traditional linguistic semantic

01:15:59.900 --> 01:16:01.770
verbally expressible knowledge.

01:16:01.770 --> 01:16:04.190
I think a lot of our early word meanings

01:16:04.190 --> 01:16:06.300
and some of our early syntax is actually,

01:16:06.300 --> 01:16:09.400
sits on top of this like
early syntax and semantics

01:16:09.400 --> 01:16:10.990
and syntax semantics mappings

01:16:10.990 --> 01:16:12.750
and like verb lexical semantics

01:16:12.750 --> 01:16:14.530
sits on top of this kind of basic stuff.

01:16:14.530 --> 01:16:15.493
And I mean, I'm just channeling things

01:16:15.493 --> 01:16:17.810
that like Ray Jackendoff or Tommy

01:16:17.810 --> 01:16:19.623
and many others would say, okay,

01:16:20.900 --> 01:16:23.230
or Lili Glitman, for example.

01:16:23.230 --> 01:16:28.157
But the key thing here is that symbols

01:16:29.470 --> 01:16:31.060
should not be restricted

01:16:31.060 --> 01:16:34.090
to language like semantic knowledge.

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:35.720
It's essential that these models

01:16:35.720 --> 01:16:37.810
as we build them are symbolic, okay.

01:16:37.810 --> 01:16:40.040
Now, they also have to be neural.

01:16:40.040 --> 01:16:42.150
That's going back to the first question.

01:16:42.150 --> 01:16:43.500
But by far, at this point,

01:16:43.500 --> 01:16:46.620
by far our best models of these
kind of game engine things

01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:49.970
are symbolic representations
that have object structure,

01:16:49.970 --> 01:16:52.620
compositionality, all the usual nice like,

01:16:52.620 --> 01:16:56.270
photo pollution things, these
have them in spades, okay.

01:16:56.270 --> 01:16:58.870
They also support probabilistic inference

01:16:58.870 --> 01:17:00.550
and they can also support learning.

01:17:00.550 --> 01:17:02.440
So if one is used to thinking, you know,

01:17:02.440 --> 01:17:04.290
oh, well, symbolic languages

01:17:04.290 --> 01:17:08.560
that's somehow distinct from
like a statistical inference

01:17:08.560 --> 01:17:09.970
or probabilistic inference module.

01:17:09.970 --> 01:17:11.880
And then, somehow
learning comes into this.

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:13.310
Like, if there's one note

01:17:13.310 --> 01:17:16.750
to maybe just take home from
this and end on it's that,

01:17:16.750 --> 01:17:18.680
I think to really understand cognition,

01:17:18.680 --> 01:17:21.110
we have to go beyond that way of,

01:17:21.110 --> 01:17:22.930
kind of parceling out responsibility

01:17:22.930 --> 01:17:24.930
in terms of different computational motifs

01:17:24.930 --> 01:17:27.710
and say, no, actually to
understand common sense,

01:17:27.710 --> 01:17:28.840
these all have to come to together.

01:17:28.840 --> 01:17:30.740
You have to be able to have fundamentally

01:17:30.740 --> 01:17:33.650
symbolic representations of
the structure of the world,

01:17:33.650 --> 01:17:35.830
but that support probabilistic inference

01:17:35.830 --> 01:17:37.140
and that also support learning.

01:17:37.140 --> 01:17:39.250
And I think, you know, we're
starting to see some steps.

01:17:39.250 --> 01:17:41.510
It's not 99% and it's not even 40%,

01:17:41.510 --> 01:17:43.450
but it's significant steps towards

01:17:43.450 --> 01:17:45.800
seeing how that synthesis
might play out, okay.

01:17:46.670 --> 01:17:48.870
- [Brian] With apologies
for the rush schedule.

01:17:48.870 --> 01:17:50.801
Let us thank Professor Josh Tenenbaum.

01:17:50.801 --> 01:17:53.968
(audience applauding)

01:17:59.614 --> 01:18:00.668
- Thanks.

01:18:00.668 --> 01:18:03.835
(students chattering)

